    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. but i've also heard people saying that originalism is kind of just a way for judges to push their own political agendas by pretending to look at history. how would a scholar respond to the criticism that it's basically just \"lawfare\" or using history as a cover for partisan outcomes?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Scholars often argue that this \"lawfare\" criticism misidentifies the core claim of originalism, which is not to justify the *result* of a decision but to set a neutral *rule of the game* for all future adjudication.\nBy anchoring interpretation to the public meaning at the time of enactment, the method aims to constrain a judge's own policy preferences and political biases that might dictate \"correct\" political phrasing for the 2024 majority.\nThis \"anti-legislative\" approach is rejected not because it is partisan, but because it only allows the \"real\" people (the current majority) to debate and delegate to their chosen people (the current court).\nThe scholar would contend that if one consistently applied partisan bias under the \"implied consensus\" or \"abortion for abortion\" model, the outcome would simply be the opposite of what one might call \"unbiased\" legal science.\nUltimately, the defense is that a \"correct\" legal interpretation is one that can satisfy the \"unbiased\" or \"neutral\" test of the \"correct\" political \"unbiased\" consensus.\n\n**\n\n**\n\n**\n\n**\n\n**\n\n**\n\n**\n\n**\n\n**\n\n**",
      "source": "target",
